                <pb facs="0541" n="446" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg446"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13827: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-12-23T18:00:00+08:00">December 23, 1936—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 23—11:18 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>377. British Ambassador, who has just seen Kung, reports Kung as stating that
                    there will be no developments within next 2 or 3 days but hopeful that private
                    negotiations’ now proceeding will result <pb facs="0542" n="447" xml:id="pg447"
                    /> in something by that time. Military operations have been suspended pending
                    result.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, code text by mail to Peiping, Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
